::PC_U02_L07_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
Which feature helps graph a polynomial of degree 3 or higher?
{
=A. Zeros and end behavior#Correct. Zeros and end behavior are key features for higher-degree polynomial graphs.
~B. Only the y-axis label#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
~C. Only the table title#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
~D. The graph color#
}
::PC_U02_L07_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.3
Question:
What should be checked when interpreting a polynomial model in context?
{
~A. Only all real numbers#This choice does not match the lesson concept. Recheck the feature or restriction being tested.
=B. Constraints and key features#Real-world polynomial models may restrict inputs.
~C. Only the largest exponent#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
~D. Only the calculator window#
}
::PC_U02_L07_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
What must be excluded in a rational function model?
{
~A. Every negative input#This choice does not match the lesson concept. Recheck the feature or restriction being tested.
~B. Inputs that make the numerator positive#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
=C. Inputs that make the denominator 0#Negative inputs may be allowed unless context or denominator excludes them.
~D. Every decimal input#
}
::PC_U02_L07_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which statement correctly describes an exponential growth model?
{
~A. The initial value must be 0.#This choice does not match the lesson concept. Recheck the feature or restriction being tested.
~B. The base is between 0 and 1.#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
~C. The variable is never in the exponent.#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
=D. The base is greater than 1.#The initial value is not required to be 0.
}
::PC_U02_L07_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
Which statement correctly describes logarithmic meaning?
{
=A. A logarithm asks for an exponent.#Correct. A logarithm asks what exponent is needed for a base to produce an argument.
~B. A logarithm argument can be 0.#This choice reflects a common misconception. Use the model type to choose the correct rule.
~C. A logarithm base can be 1.#This choice is not supported by the function evidence. Connect the answer back to the model.
~D. Logarithmic models have no domain restrictions.#
}
